Theory:
SJF Algorithm
Shortest Job First (SJF) is a non-preemptive scheduling algorithm that selects the process with the
shortest burst time (execution time) to execute next. It is also known as Shortest Job Next (SJN).
Working Principle:
 At any scheduling point, the CPU is assigned to the process with the smallest burst time.
 If two processes have the same burst time, FCFS (First Come First Serve) is used as a tie-breaker.
 Once a process starts execution, it runs to completion (non-preemptive).
Steps:
1. Sort all processes by burst time.
2. Execute the process with the shortest burst time.
3. Calculate:
o Waiting Time (WT) = Start Time – Arrival Time
o Turnaround Time (TAT) = Completion Time – Arrival Time
FCFS Algorithm
First-Come, First-Served (FCFS) is the simplest CPU scheduling algorithm, where the process that 
arrives first gets executed first. It follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle, like a queue. 
 Waiting Time (WT) = Start Time − Arrival Time
 Turnaround Time (TAT) = Completion Time − Arrival Time
 TAT = WT + Burst Time
Working Principle:
1. Processes are added to the ready queue in the order they arrive.
2. CPU picks the first process in the queue and runs it until completion.
3. No preemption – once a process starts, it runs till it finishes.
 Priority Based Algorithm
Priority Scheduling is a CPU scheduling algorithm where each process is assigned a priority, and the
CPU is allocated to the process with the highest priority.
